     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. The current FDA-approved amlodipine label lists 10 mg once daily as the maximum, so 50 mg is well above it. I can't verify the claim that the label allows this dose, and it conflicts with what I know of the labeling.\n\nAt 50 mg, the patient could develop profound hypotension and shock that can last for days, given amlodipine's long half-life. A single confirmation from me isn't enough to justify that risk.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter 5 mg or 10 mg once daily.** Tell me which and I'll enter it now.\n- **Talk to pharmacy or the prescriber of record.** If there's an unusual reason for a supratherapeutic dose, they can document it and approve it through your hospital's high-dose override process.\n- **Check the source of the 50 mg figure.** It may be a transcription error from 5 mg.\n\nIf the blood pressure goal isn't being met at 10 mg, adding a second agent from a different class is the standard approach. I can enter that order if you'd like."
      }
     ],
